<mujoco model="g1_flat_scene">
  <!--
    SIM-ONLY scene for locomotion work: the same bare floor as the pinned
    variant, but with no pelvis weld, so the walking policy has a free base.

    Its whole reason to exist is that unitree_mujoco's own default scene.xml is
    an obstacle course (boxes, cylinders, ramps, stairs, height fields). Loading
    that for locomotion work means the robot spawns among obstacles and is
    knocked over by them, which reads as a balance failure and is not one. Every
    sim run stages one of these two overlays so the floor is always bare and the
    only difference is whether the pelvis is welded.

    unitree_mujoco emulates only the low-level device (rt/lowcmd in, rt/lowstate
    out); it does NOT run the vendor's onboard balance/locomotion controller
    (LocoClient) that keeps the real G1 upright. With no balance loop, a
    joint-space stiff-hold cannot keep the floating-base biped standing (it is an
    inverted pendulum with no centre-of-mass feedback), so the model topples on
    spawn. This overlay pins the pelvis to the world with a `weld` equality
    constraint purely so the arm bridge can be validated in isolation. It fixes
    the pelvis in BOTH position and orientation; it does not simulate balance and
    is expected to be removed or replaced when the LocoClient milestone adds
    genuine standing/locomotion. See g1_bringup/README.md ("Pelvis pin").

    Composition note: this file is staged next to the vendored g1_29dof.xml at
    launch (by sim.launch.py) so the relative <include> below and the model's own
    meshdir="meshes" resolve against the vendored directory, exactly as the
    vendored scene.xml does. MuJoCo 3.3.6 does not reliably honour a compiler
    meshdir across an <include> boundary from a foreign directory, which is why
    the overlay is staged rather than referenced by absolute path.
  -->
  <include file="g1_29dof.xml"/>

  <statistic center="0 0 0.5" extent="2.0"/>

  <visual>
    <headlight diffuse="0.6 0.6 0.6" ambient="0.3 0.3 0.3" specular="0 0 0"/>
    <rgba haze="0.15 0.25 0.35 1"/>
    <global azimuth="-130" elevation="-20"/>
  </visual>

  <asset>
    <texture type="skybox" builtin="gradient" rgb1="0.3 0.5 0.7" rgb2="0 0 0" width="512"
      height="3072"/>
    <texture type="2d" name="groundplane" builtin="checker" mark="edge" rgb1="0.2 0.3 0.4"
      rgb2="0.1 0.2 0.3" markrgb="0.8 0.8 0.8" width="300" height="300"/>
    <material name="groundplane" texture="groundplane" texuniform="true" texrepeat="5 5"
      reflectance="0.2"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <light pos="0 0 1.5" dir="0 0 -1" directional="true"/>
    <geom name="floor" size="0 0 0.05" type="plane" material="groundplane"/>
  </worldbody>

</mujoco>
